  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bauxite is hydrated aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃·2H₂O, and it is the only ore used commercially.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium oxide is amphoteric, so it dissolves in hot alkali as sodium aluminate; iron(III) oxide does not.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iron(III) oxide does not dissolve in alkali and is filtered off as red mud.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2Al(OH)₃ → Al₂O₃ + 3H₂O at about 1200 °C; the product is pure alumina for the cell.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cryolite is sodium hexafluoroaluminate, and it is used as the solvent for alumina in the Hall–Héroult cell.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Alumina melts above 2000 °C; dissolved in cryolite it can be electrolysed at about 950 °C.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The molten cryolite–alumina mixture is kept at about 950 °C.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cathode is negative and supplies electrons, reducing the aluminium ion to the metal.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oxide ions lose electrons at the carbon anodes, and the oxygen released burns the carbon away.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C + O₂ → CO₂ at the anode, so the anodes are consumed; replacing them is a major running cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A reducing agent must be above the metal; carbon is below aluminium and cannot take its oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cell decomposes alumina into aluminium and oxygen; the oxygen then burns the anodes.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium conducts well and is light, so a cable can span a long distance without excessive sag.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The aluminium is made the anode in a dilute acid, and a thicker, harder oxide layer grows on its surface.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Brass is copper with zinc; bronze, by contrast, is copper with tin.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ore supplies the iron, coke supplies heat and carbon monoxide, and limestone removes the silica.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Limestone decomposes to calcium oxide, which combines with silica to form calcium silicate slag.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Calcium oxide combines with silicon(IV) oxide to give calcium silicate, CaSiO₃.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂; the carbon is present as the source of the CO, not as the reducing agent itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C + O₂ → CO₂ is strongly exothermic and provides the heat of the furnace; higher up, the CO₂ is reduced to CO.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Carbon dioxide rising from the bottom meets red-hot coke in the middle of the furnace and is reduced to carbon monoxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Pig iron contains about 4 % carbon, which makes it hard and brittle and requires refining into steel.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mild steel is soft, ductile and easy to weld, so it is used for car bodies, nails and pipes.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    High-carbon steel is very hard, which is what a cutting edge needs, at the cost of brittleness.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Above about 2 % carbon the material is cast iron: hard, brittle and not forgeable.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chromium forms a protective Cr₂O₃ skin, and nickel improves toughness and corrosion resistance.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Manganese steel is exceptionally tough and wear-resistant, which is why it is used for track work.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Tungsten steels retain their hardness when they become hot, which is essential for high-speed cutting.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc is above iron and corrodes in its place, even where the coating is scratched.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc is more reactive than the steel, so it corrodes first and protects the hull by sacrificial action.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The tin acts as a cathode and concentrates the corrosion on the exposed iron.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    M(Al₂O₃) = 102, so n = 1.00 mol; 2Al₂O₃ gives 4Al, so 2.00 mol of aluminium is obtained, which has a mass of 54 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    54 ÷ 102 × 100 = 52.9 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    M(Fe₂O₃) = 160, so 1.00 × 10⁶ mol gives 2.00 × 10⁶ mol of iron, and 2 × 56 = 112 t.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1.00 mol of Fe₂O₃ needs 3.00 mol of CO, and 3 × 28 = 84 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂, so 1.00 mol of lime needs 1.00 mol of limestone, and 1.00 × 100 = 100 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium oxide reacts with acids to give salts and with alkalis to give aluminates.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Al₂O₃ layer seals the surface and prevents further attack.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The anodes burn away in the oxygen released at the positive electrode and must be replaced continuously.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The opposing flows exchange heat efficiently: the rising gas preheats the falling charge and leaves cooler.
  ]

]

